      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Al-Anon self-help group is the sole source of assistance for families whose members suffer from alcohol abuse problems in Iran."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To assess the effects of Al-Anon meeting attendance on family function and quality of life of women living with husbands with alcohol use disorders."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study was conducted among two groups of women first time participants in Al-Anon (n\u2009=\u200940) and participants who had attended Al-Anon for at least six months (n\u2009=\u200940) in Mashhad, Iran. Participants were administered the Quality of Life Short Form (QOL SF-36) and Family Assessment Device (FAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was a significant group difference in the QOL SF-36 total as well as in the sub-scores of role limitations due to physical problems, role limitations due to emotional problems, vitality, pain, and social functioning sub-scores. No significant group differences were observed in FAD sub-scores, except for the subscale of problem solving."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These findings suggest Al-Anon, when other structured therapeutic interventions are not available, may be of particular benefit for quality of life issues in women attendees."
        }
      ]},
